Evidence existed everywhere. Yet the Towers still fell.

In 2004, response to the terrorist attacks in New York City, Virginia, and Pennsylvania the 9/11 Commission recommended greater data sharing to ensure the clues would never get overlooked again.

In response, federal agencies created the Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI) program. In 2010 President Obama signed Executive Order 13556 which charged the Government with developing and defining the program.

The National Archives and Records  Administration defined CUI  as any data created by or for the government that requires "safeguarding or dissemination controls pursuant to and consistent with  applicable law, regulations, and government-wide policies.

Basically if someone wrote a rule or law that says, "Hey you must do X when handling Y" it is CUI.

On May 8, 2015, proposed rule 32 CFR Part 2002 was published in the  Federal Register and entered the Office of Management and Budget  (OMB)-managed Federal regulatory process.

Fifteen years after improper data labeling and sharing lead to the greatest attack on American soil since Pearl Harbor,  Rule 32 CFR Part 2002 Controlled Unclassified Information got published.

The CUI program sought to increase data sharing to prevent another 9/11 by what Barack Obama noted would required a program that "emphasizes the openness and uniformity of Government-wide practice."

